{"subjectId":"javascript","id":"callbacks","title":"Callbacks & Callback Hell","group":"Asynchronous JavaScript","order":42,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">The original async pattern: pass a function to be called when the work is done. Simple — until you nest five of them.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-circle\"></i>Error-first callbacks (Node convention)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function readUser(id, callback) {\n  setTimeout(() =&gt; {\n    if (id &lt;= 0) callback(new Error(\"invalid id\"));\n    else callback(null, { id, name: \"User\" + id });\n  }, 10);\n}\nreadUser(7, (err, user) =&gt; {\n  if (err) return console.log(\"failed:\", err.message);\n  console.log(\"got\", user);\n});\nreadUser(-1, (err, user) =&gt; {\n  if (err) return console.log(\"failed:\", err.message);\n  console.log(\"got\", user);\n});</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>got { id: 7, name: 'User7' }\nfailed: invalid id</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stairs-down\"></i>Callback hell (the pyramid of doom)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const step = (name, cb) =&gt; setTimeout(() =&gt; cb(null, name), 5);\nstep(\"login\", (err, a) =&gt; {\n  step(\"load profile\", (err, b) =&gt; {\n    step(\"load orders\", (err, c) =&gt; {\n      step(\"render\", (err, d) =&gt; {\n        console.log([a, b, c, d].join(\" → \"));\n      });\n    });\n  });\n});</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>login → load profile → load orders → render</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Why it hurts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Nesting</td><td>Code drifts right; hard to read and refactor</td></tr><tr><td>Error handling</td><td>Must check <code>err</code> at every level; <code>try/catch</code> cannot catch errors thrown in later callbacks</td></tr><tr><td>Inversion of control</td><td>You trust the callee to call you exactly once, not zero or twice, not sync <em>and</em> async</td></tr><tr><td>Composition</td><td>Parallel work, timeouts and \"first one wins\" need hand-written bookkeeping</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-transform\"></i>Promisify</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>import { promisify } from \"node:util\";\nfunction legacyAdd(a, b, cb) { setTimeout(() =&gt; cb(null, a + b), 5); }\n\nconst addAsync = promisify(legacyAdd);\nconsole.log(await addAsync(2, 3));\n\nconst manual = (a, b) =&gt;\n  new Promise((resolve, reject) =&gt; legacyAdd(a, b, (err, v) =&gt; (err ? reject(err) : resolve(v))));\nconsole.log(await manual(10, 20));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>5\n30</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is callback hell and how do you avoid it?</summary><div><p>Deeply nested callbacks for sequential async steps, which make code hard to read, reason about and handle errors in. Avoid it with named functions, promises and chaining, and most of all <code>async</code>/<code>await</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}